% [Actual \u2014 FRED/GovSpending], tight. No distress signal.\n\n1-2. Regime classification\nRegime probability distribution:\n\u2022 Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 25%\n\u2022 Reflation (high rates + growth): 45%\n\u2022 Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 20%\n\u2022 Recession (low rates + low growth): 10%\nTotal = 100%. (Justification: strong ISM with 10Y at 4.7% is classic Reflation; Fed easing bias keeps a Goldilocks tail; late-cycle risk keeps a Stagflation/Recession tail.)\n\n1-3. Enterprise macro sensitivity (5 axes, -3 to +3)\n\u2022 Interest rate sensitivity: -2 (industrial capex, yield competition for equity)\n\u2022 Dollar sensitivity: -1 (mostly domestic; Canadian exposure modest)\n\u2022 Liquidity sensitivity: -1 (self-funding; not dependent on credit)\n\u2022 Economic cycle sensitivity: -2.5 (MRO is sticky but OEM/automation project spend is cyclical)\n\u2022 Commodity sensitivity: -1 (steel/inventory input; LIFO effects)\n\n1-4. Macro alignment judgment\nRegime probability \u00d7 sensitivity \u2192 Expected macro impact \u2248 -0.1 to +0.2 \u2192 NEUTRAL.\nMacro is not a tailwind at 4.7% rates. ISM strength supports near-term revenue, but late-cycle positioning means the base case must embed cyclical risk. Conviction change: 0.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n1.5-1. Base rates applied\n\u2022 5-year survival of a $5B revenue industrial distributor: >95% [Base rate].\n\u2022 Probability a distributor doubles to $7B revenue in 5 years (7.1% CAGR) organically + small M&A: ~25-35% [Base rate \u2014 most 5-year plans are stretched].\n\u2022 M&A at >$500M scale achieving ROIC > WACC: ~30-40% [Base rate].\n\u2022 Cyclical peak-to-40%-drawdown within 3 years for industrial cyclicals: ~30-40% [Base rate].\n\u2022 Management 5-yr plan ($7B / 14% EBITDA) delivered in full: ~30% [Assumption \u2014 applying the base rate to a plan that embeds M&A].\n\n1.5-2. Deviation from base rate\nMy base case (5.3% revenue CAGR, EBITDA margin 13.9% by FY2031) deviates from the optimistic base rate by being more conservative. This is justified by three company-specific facts:\n   1. AIT has grown revenue at 4.0% CAGR FY2023-26 (4.413\u21924.967B); the $7B target needs 7.1%, a 75% acceleration.\n   2. Current EBITDA margin (12.45%) is already far above the 12-year median (7.0%) [pack SECTION 7]; the 14% target implies further structural mix shift.\n   3. M&A contribution is explicitly not in FY2027 guidance \u2014 the plan depends on deals we cannot forecast.\n\n1.5-3. Base rate shock check\nNo successful probability in my model exceeds 1.5x the base rate (bull case 20% < 1.5 \u00d7 30% base rate of full-plan delivery). PASS.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n2-1. Business understanding\nAIT is a value-added distributor of industrial motion control, fluid power, flow control, automation and maintenance components, serving MRO and OEM demand across North America via ~600 branches. It generates ~$5.0B revenue at 30.3% gross margin by bundling technical engineering services with product distribution. Understandability: HIGH.\n\n2-2. Moat assessment (0-3 each)\n\u2022 Intangible assets: 2 (technical expertise, supplier relationships, ~100-year brand)\n\u2022 Switching costs: 2.5 (plant-floor MRO disruption risk; training and legacy specs)\n\u2022 Network effects: 0.5 (branch density created modest logistics advantage, not true network effects)\n\u2022 Cost advantage: 1.5 (largest independent; no scale advantage vs Grainger/Fastenal)\n\u2022 Economies of scale: 2.0 (branch network fixed-cost leverage)\n\u2022 Regulatory barriers: 0.5 (low)\n\u2022 Data moat: 1.5 (customer spend data, automation install base; nascent)\nTOTAL: 10.5 / 21 \u2192 NARROW moat, at the upper boundary.\nMoat direction: STABLE-TO-WIDENING \u2014 evidence: share gains via \"One Applied\" cross-sell; Engineered Solutions organic growth 12.9% in Q4 FY2026 [Actual \u2014 press release]; automation sales +20% organic [Actual \u2014 call transcript]; National Strategic Accounts +8% [Actual \u2014 call].\n\n2-3. Lynch classification: STALWART\nSteady mid-single-digit organic growth, strong FCF, low leverage, 0.58% dividend yield [Aggregator]. Not a fast grower: organic growth 5.4% in FY2026 [Actual \u2014 press release].\n\n2-4. Financial strength scorecard\n\u2022 Revenue growth 3-yr CAGR (FY2023-26): 4.0% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 Operating margin latest: 11.1%; 12-yr median 7.0% [pack]\n\u2022 Net margin: 8.4% FY2026 [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 ROE: 22.4% [Aggregator]\n\u2022 FCF FY2026: $461M [pack]; FCF conversion vs NI \u2248 111% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 Net debt: -$127M cash + $262M LT debt + $221M leases = $356M net debt [my definition, Arithmetic]; net leverage 0.2x EBITDA [Actual \u2014 company]\n\u2022 FY2026 D&A: $66M; capex: $24M (property/plant only) [pack]\nJudgment: EXCELLENT.\n\n2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0-3, total 15)\n   1. Buyback efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 repurchased 1.2M shares for $317M in FY2026, avg ~$264/share (\u224824x FY26 EPS); good but not at valuation lows.\n   2. M&A track record: 2/3 \u2014 Hydrodyne double-digit sales growth and +200bp EBITDA margins [Actual \u2014 call]; no impairments disclosed; not a 3 because scale of record is limited.\n   3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 incremental ROIC >20% historically; FY2027 capex step-up to $35-40M is a new test.\n   4. Rationality of mix: 3/3 \u2014 buybacks, dividend, low leverage (0.2x EBITDA [Actual \u2014 company]); capacity for M&A being preserved.\n   5. Guidance accuracy: 2/3 \u2014 Q4 beat; FY2026 beat; but 1H FY2026 saw an EPS miss per the web brief [Estimate \u2014 unverified weatherapi secondary].\n   Insider ownership: 0.76% \u2014 no bonus [pack].\n   Score: 11/15. GOOD.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n\u2022 R&D capitalization: minimal R&D for a distributor; no adjustment.\n\u2022 SBC treatment: FY2026 SBC $13M [pack]. Adjusted FCF = $461M - $13M = $448M [Arithmetic]. Owner earnings = NI $415M + D&A $66M - maintenance capex (\u2248$30M [Assumption]) - \u0394WC (operating; near zero ex-cash) \u2248 $451M [Estimate].\n\u2022 Operating lease capitalization: $221M (FY2026) added to debt [pack SECTION 6B].\n\u2022 Adjusted net debt = $262M LT debt + $221M leases - $127M cash = $356M [Arithmetic].\n\u2022 Adjusted ROIC = NOPAT / (equity + debt + leases - cash) = $415M / $2.218B \u2248 18.7% [Arithmetic]. GAAP ROIC \u2248 20.8% [Arithmetic]. The 2.1pp gap is from lease capitalization \u2014 not a red flag.\n\u2022 No brand/data asset adjustment (distributor; brand value already in margins).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 1 (DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n3-1. Discount rate derivation [all components]\n\u2022 Rf = 4.71% [Actual \u2014 10Y UST, TradingEconomics 2026-08-21]\n\u2022 ERP = 4.75% [Assumption \u2014 implied/long-run Damodaran range 4.5-5.0%]\n\u2022 Beta = 0.90 [Assumption \u2014 vendor beta 0.847 with unknown window, blended toward 1.0 for cyclicality; if vendor beta alone, CoE would be 8.74%]\n\u2022 Cost of equity = 4.71% + 0.90 \u00d7 4.75% = 8.99%\n\u2022 Pre-tax cost of debt = 5.5% [Assumption \u2014 investment-grade distributor; 10Y + ~80bp]\n\u2022 After-tax Kd = 5.5% \u00d7 (1 - 25%) [tax assumption] = 4.13%\n\u2022 Weights: E = 97.2%, D = 2.8% (market cap 12.4B vs net debt 0.36B)\n\u2022 WACC = 8.99% \u00d7 0.972 + 4.13% \u00d7 0.028 = 8.9% [Arithmetic]\n\n3-2. Base-case forecast FY2027-31 (all $M except per-share)\n\nForecast drivers - sourced:\n\u2022 FY2027 revenue: +4.0-6.5% [Actual \u2014 company guidance]; I use midpoint +5.25%.\n\u2022 FY2027 EBITDA margin: 12.5-12.8% [Actual \u2014 company guidance]; midpoint 12.65%.\n\u2022 FY2027 capex: $35-40M [Actual \u2014 company guidance]; midpoint $38M. This is a 58% step-up from the pack's $24M capex (property/plant only) \u2014 the company is investing in automation footprint.\n\u2022 FY2027 D&A: \u2248$72M [Estimate \u2014 pack FY2026 $66M plus higher capex and intangibles amortization].\n\u2022 FY2027 tax: 25% [Assumption \u2014 derived effective rate 24.4% FY2026; rounded]\n\u2022 FY2027 interest: $12-13M [Actual \u2014 company guidance]; I use $13M for EPS cross-check; valuation uses NOPAT.\n\u2022 FY2028-31 revenue: NOT guided beyond FY27. Path is my [Assumption]: +5.5% / +6.0% / +5.5% / +5.0% \u2014 this is 5.3% CAGR FY26-31, consistent with historical organic (5.4% FY2026 [Actual]) plus modest M&A, and deliberately below management's $7B target (7.1% CAGR) which I treat as the bull case. I show valuation sensitivity to this range (Section 3-4).\n\u2022 FY2028-31 EBITDA margin: 13.0% / 13.3% / 13.6% / 13.9% [Assumption \u2014 interpolated toward the 14% management target by FY2031 (the target years out at \"next five years\" [Actual \u2014 press release])].\n\u2022 FY2028-31 capex: $40M / $42M / $44M / $46M [Assumption \u2014 follows guided step-up, grows with business].\n\u2022 Working capital: OWC = 14.7% of revenue [Arithmetic \u2014 (AR 0.831 + Inv 0.509 - CL 0.612) / 4.967 FY2026]; \u0394WC = 14.7% \u00d7 \u0394Revenue.\n\u2022 Terminal: g = 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 below nominal GDP; justified by distributor maturity]. Terminal D&A = capex at maintenance ($85M). Terminal \u0394WC = 0.45% of revenue (15% of 3% growth).\n\nForecast table (revenue, EBITDA%, EBITDA, D&A, EBIT, tax 25% \u2192 NOPAT, capex, \u0394WC, FCFF):\n\nFY2026A: Rev 4,967; EBITDA% 12.45%; EBITDA 618; D&A 69; EBIT 549; NOPAT 415; Capex 24\nFY2027E: Rev 5,228; EBITDA% 12.65%; EBITDA 661; D&A 72; EBIT 589; NOPAT 442; Capex 38; \u0394WC 39; FCFF 437\nFY2028E: Rev 5,516; EBITDA% 13.0%;  EBITDA 717; D&A 76; EBIT 641; NOPAT 481; Capex 40; \u0394WC 43; FCFF 474\nFY2029E: Rev 5,847; EBITDA% 13.3%;  EBITDA 778; D&A 80; EBIT 698; NOPAT 524; Capex 42; \u0394WC 50; FCFF 512\nFY2030E: Rev 6,169; EBITDA% 13.6%;  EBITDA 839; D&A 82; EBIT 757; NOPAT 568; Capex 44; \u0394WC 48; FCFF 558\nFY2031E: Rev 6,477; EBITDA% 13.9%;  EBITDA 900; D&A 85; EBIT 815; NOPAT 611; Capex 46; \u0394WC 46; FCFF 604\n\nTerminal (FY2031+): NOPAT grows at 3% = 629; terminal FCFF = 629 - 30 (\u0394WC) = 599.\nTV = 599 / (8.9% - 3.0%) = 10,153.\n\n3-3. DCF result\nPV(FCFF FY27-31) = 437/1.089 + 474/1.089\u00b2 + 512/1.089\u00b3 + 558/1.089\u2074 + 604/1.089\u2075 = 401 + 400 + 397 + 397 + 393 = 1,988.\nPV(TV) = 10,153 / 1.089\u2075 = 6,568.\nEV = 8,556.\nLess: net debt = $356M (LT debt $262M + operating leases $221M - cash $127M) [my definition; current portion assumed $0M since FY2025 current portion was $0M and vendor total-debt of $572M vs our $483M leaves a $89M gap [Unconfirmed \u2014 unresolved, immaterial ~1% of EV]].\nEquity = 8,200. Forward diluted shares \u2248 36.5M [Estimate \u2014 36.7M outstanding, net 0.2M buyback next year].\nBASE-CASE INTRINSIC VALUE = $224 per share [Estimate, 50% CI $190-280; 80% CI $145-340].\n\n3-4. Valuation sensitivity \u2014 post-guidance period\nThe FY2028-31 path is NOT established by guidance. The IV across the plausible range:\n\n  5-yr Revenue CAGR   FY2031 EBITDA margin   IV/share\n  4.0%                13.0%                  ~$175\n  5.3% (base)         13.9%                  ~$224\n  6.0%                14.0%                  ~$255\n  7.1% (hits $7B)     14.0%                  ~$285\n\nCurrent price of $338 implies \u22487% revenue CAGR AND 14%+ EBITDA margin AND a terminal value at or above these assumptions \u2014 i.e., the market is pricing delivery at the very top of the plausible range.\n\n3-5. Reverse DCF (market-implied)\nAt $337.93: EV = 12,402 + 356 = 12,758. With my explicit-period PV (~2,000) and same WACC 8.9%, the implied TV is \u224816,600 \u2192 implied terminal FCFF \u2248 980. On my FY2031 revenue, that requires a NOPAT margin \u224815% (EBIT margin \u224820%) \u2014 far above any level ever achieved at AIT. Alternatively, the market is assuming WACC \u22488.0-8.5% and g\u22484% \u2014 a permanent low-rate world plus a multi-decade growth runway. Either path is fragile.\n\n3-6. Multiples cross-check\n\u2022 FY2027 EPS midpoint $11.90 [Actual \u2014 guidance]. At $337.93: forward P/E 28.4x.\n\u2022 Trailing P/E 31.8x [Aggregator]; PEG 3.09 [Aggregator] \u2014 expensive for 8.8% EPS CAGR.\n\u2022 EV/EBITDA FY2026: 12,758/618 = 20.6x [Arithmetic] \u2014 rich for a 12.5% EBITDA-margin distribution business.\n\u2022 Consensus targets mean $400/median $400 [Aggregator] \u2014 implies 18% upside; consensus is a lagging indicator, not a valuation.\n\u2022 Earnings yield 3.5% vs 10Y 4.71% \u2014 the market pays you less yield than the risk-free rate, requiring perfect execution.\nMultiples do NOT support the price; they confirm the DCF.\n\n3-X. Tornado (Top 3 variables = Thesis KPIs)\n   1. FY2031 EBITDA margin (\u00b150bp) \u2192 IV \u00b1$20\n   2. Revenue CAGR FY28-31 (\u00b11pp) \u2192 IV \u00b1$35\n   3. WACC (\u00b150bp) \u2192 IV \u00b1$30\nThese three variables appear in Section 8 monitoring.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n3.5-1. Competitive response: If AIT's automation/technical push succeeds, Grainger, Fastenal, Motion and MSC will escalate price competition and vending/tech investment, pressuring the incremental returns (\u22121 on serverity). Fastenal's branch/vending footprint remains a direct threat [Estimate \u2014 web brief].\n3.5-2. Reflexivity: High stock price gives AIT cheap equity for M&A currency (positive); but a de-rating would hit a 98.6% institutionally-held, low-insider-ownership stock harder (negative). Net: neutral-to-negative.\n3.5-3. Customer behavior: The \"One Applied\" strategy shifts customers to multi-category technical solutions; LTV/CAC improves as strategic accounts grow (+8% organic [Actual \u2014 call]). As penetration of automation rises, the marginal large-account win gets harder. Neutral.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4. SCENAR