00\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Understanding (3 sentences): AIT distributes bearings, power-\ntransmission, fluid-power, flow-control, automation and MRO supplies to North\nAmerican industrial plants, earning a ~30% gross margin by combining branch\nscale (~600 service centers) with engineering services (fabrication, repair,\nautomation integration). It monetizes an installed base of industrial\ncustomers two ways \u2014 recurring MRO replenishment (defensive) and project-based\nengineered solutions (growth, higher margin). End markets: food/beverage,\nmetals, aggregate/cement, energy, chemicals, plus fast-growing technology/\ndata-center exposure.\nUnderstandability: HIGH. (No downgrade.)\n\nStep 2-2. Moat score (0\u201321) + direction:\n  Intangible assets (technical brand, OEM certifications)  2\n  Switching costs (MRO replenishment, NSAs, ES contracts)  3\n  Network effects (branch density, One Applied cross-sell) 1\n  Cost advantage (scale buying, 600-branch density)        2\n  Economies of scale (SG&A 18.6% of sales, \u221294bps y/y)     2\n  Regulatory barriers (safety/industrial standards)        1\n  Data moat (customer/asset analytics, automation data)    2\n  TOTAL: 13/21 \u2192 WIDE moat (borderline narrow/wide).\n  Direction: STABLE-to-WIDENING \u2014 ES mix, strategic accounts (+8% organic),\n  automation attach and technical MRO demand are widening the moat; competing\n  against FAST/GWW/Motion/MSM who are all investing in automation keeps\n  pressure on. (Note: one low-quality web source claims \"no economic moat\";\n  our scored assessment is 13/21 and is based on 12 yrs of filed margin and\n  share data \u2014 treat the web claim as [Unconfirmed] and inferior to this.)\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification: STALWART with fast-grower optionality.\n  Revenue CAGR 5-yr 8.9%, EPS ~7\u201310% growth, recurring MRO base \u2192 Stalwart.\n  Checkpoint: P/E 28.4x forward vs ~10% growth \u2192 PEG ~2.9 \u2192 FAILS the classic\n  Stalwart \"P/E \u2248 growth rate\" rule. This is the valuation red flag.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard (all [Actual] unless noted):\n  Revenue growth 3-yr CAGR: FY23\u219226 = (4,967/4,413)^(1/3)\u22121 = +4.0% [Arithmetic]\n  Revenue growth last 4Q avg: ~+8% (Q4 FY26 +10.4%) [Arithmetic]\n  Operating margin FY2026 11.06%; 3-yr avg 10.9%; 12-yr median 7.0% [Arithmetic]\n  ROE 22.4% [Estimate \u2014 vendor]; ROIC ~18.8% [Estimate \u2014 see \u00a72.5]\n  FCF margin 9.3% (461/4,967); FCF/OCF conversion 95% [Arithmetic]\n  Net debt / EBITDA: (262+221\u2212127)/618 = 0.58x incl. leases; 0.2x per company\n    [Arithmetic/Actual]\n  Interest coverage: EBITDA/(interest+lease interest) \u2248 618/47 \u2248 13x [Estimate]\n  Accruals ratio \u22122.25% (earnings more cash-backed than reported) [Arithmetic]\n  Piotroski 8/9 [Arithmetic]\n  Verdict: EXCELLENT.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0\u201315):\n  Buyback efficiency: FY2026 1.2M sh for $317M at ~24x TTM EPS \u2014 good size,\n    but not cheap (above 10-yr avg multiple) \u2192 2/3\n  M&A track record: bolt-ons accretive (Hydrodyne +200bps margin), but no\n    large-deal proof \u2192 2/3\n  Capex efficiency: $24M capex supported $400M revenue growth; incremental\n    ROIC >> 20% \u2192 3/3\n  Mix rationality: buybacks + dividend + debt paydown + $2B dry powder,\n    balanced \u2192 3/3\n  Guidance accuracy: beat FY2026, raised FY2027, raised targets \u2192 3/3\n  TOTAL 13/15. Insider bonus: insider ownership 0.76% \u2014 no bonus; no insider\n  cluster-buy data held [Unconfirmed]. Score 13/15 \u2192 EXCELLENT.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization: immaterial for a distributor (no R&D line\nheld) [Unconfirmed \u2014 no R&D in pack]; no adjustment.\nStep 2.5-2. SBC: FY2026 $13M (0.26% of revenue) \u2014 treat as full cash expense:\n  Adjusted FCF = 461 \u2212 13 = $448M [Estimate]; SBC dilution is minimal and\n  already offset by 2.5%/yr net buyback.\nStep 2.5-3. Operating leases: $221M capitalized (FY2026). Adjusted net debt =\n  262 (LT debt) + 221 (leases) \u2212 127 (cash) = $356M = 0.58x EBITDA [Arithmetic]\n  \u2014 note total-debt composition is [Unconfirmed] (pack cannot verify whether\n  the LT-debt columns overlap; we used LT debt noncurrent + operating leases\n  and state that choice).\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data assets: distributor brand value not material; data\n  moat embedded in One Applied/cross-sell \u2014 not capitalized (conservative).\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: NOPAT (549.5 \u00d7 0.76) = $417.6M / Invested capital\n  (equity 1,862 + debt 262 + leases 221 \u2212 cash 127 = 2,218) = 18.8% [Estimate]\n  vs GAAP ROE 22.4% [Estimate]. Gap < 5pp \u2192 no hidden asset base to adjust.\n  ROIC ~19% vs WACC ~8.4% \u2192 spread >10pp, sustained 5 yrs \u2192 genuine compounder.\nStep 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett): NI 414.5 + D&A 66 \u2212 maintenance capex\n  30 [Assumption] = $450.5M \u2212 SBC 13 = ~$438M [Estimate]. FCF conversion\n  (OCF/NI) 1.17x [Arithmetic]. AIT is a cash machine; this is not the issue.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 3. VALUATION\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 0-2/0-3/0-4. Engine selection:\n  Archetype: C (Cyclical) \u2192 B (Quality Compounder) TRANSITION, ~65\u201370% of\n  transition criteria met: ROIC > WACC for 5 consecutive years (criterion\n  met); margin volatility collapsed (FY22\u201326 OI range 9.4\u201311.1% vs FY15\u201321\n  range 2.7\u20137.4% \u2014 met); revenue growth positive through weak ISM 2023\u201325\n  (met); but the business remains 3\u00d7 cycle-sensitive to US industrial\n  production (not met). 50\u201380% criteria \u2192 BLENDED ENGINE:\n  Engine 1 (Traditional DCF) 65% + Engine 2 (Cycle-Adjusted) 35%.\n  Alternative-engine error: pure Engine 1 overpays for cyclicality; pure\n  Engine 2 (mid-cycle normalization at historical multiples) undervalues the\n  demonstrated structural margin step-up. Engine 4/5 inappropriate \u2014 AIT is\n  cash-generative; optionality is material but subordinate to core value.\n\n  WACC derivation (base):\n    rf = 4.7% (10Y, 08/21/26) [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics]\n    ERP = 4.5% (long-run US equity premium) [Assumption]\n    beta = 0.85 [Estimate \u2014 vendor, window unstated]\n    Cost of equity = 4.7 + 0.85 \u00d7 4.5 = 8.53% [Arithmetic]\n    Cost of debt = 5.0% pre-tax [Assumption], 3.8% after-tax (24%)\n    Weights: E 96.3% / D 3.7% (mkt: 12,402 / 483 incl. leases)\n    WACC = 0.963 \u00d7 8.53 + 0.037 \u00d7 3.80 = 8.4%  \u2192 base 8.4%, stress 7.5\u20139.5%\n  (If ERP 4.0% \u2192 WACC ~7.9%; if ERP 5.0% \u2192 WACC ~8.8%. 8.4% is the center.)\n\n  FORECAST PATH (base case; $M except margins) \u2014 every driver sourced:\n  \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n  \u2502 FY   \u2502Revenue \u2502 OI%  \u2502 D&A  \u2502Capex \u2502 \u0394NWC  \u2502 FCFF \u2502Sourced from \u2502\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25